.TP
.B qargi local
Prepare, verify and clean a local run that has no way to push.
.PP
The agent works in a clone of a bare copy that has no remote, runs git through a credential\-less wrapper, and verify compares every ref of the real repository before and after. It is a guard, not a sandbox.
.RS
.TP
.B prepare
Make the clone and the wrapper for a local agent.
.br
Prints RUN, ROLE, CLASS, CLONE, OUT, TMP, GIT and BASE_SHA, then a blank line and a TASK\-BLOCK PATHS block (RUN_DIR, CLONE, OUT, TMP, GIT, BASE_SHA) to copy into the task. A caller that evals the output must stop at the blank line.
When the project (found by matching the repository against the stored answers) has a Claude Code render (packs apply \-\-target claude\-code) that has drifted, prepare also prints a WARNING block to stderr with the findings and the fix, packs apply \-\-target claude\-code PROJECT. The run goes on, and stdout and the TASK\-BLOCK PATHS block are unchanged.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo " "DIR"
the local repository.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-repo\-url " "URL"
clone a private repository from this URL.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-credential\-file " "FILE"
token file for \-\-repo\-url.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-base " "BRANCH"
the base branch.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-slug " "SLUG"
the task slug (branch agent/<slug>).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-scratch " "DIR"
where to put the run.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-role " "ROLE"
the task's role, written to RUN/ability with its class in RUN/class; a read\-only role keeps the clone unchanged (default build).
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-profile " "NAME"
the profile to dispatch under (for example advisor or auditor for review); resolved before any clone, refused when its class does not fit the role, recorded in RUN/profile and printed with MEMBER, MODEL and EFFORT.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-ability " "ABILITY"
the earlier name of \-\-role.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "verify " "RUN_DIR"
Compare the repository's refs with the snapshot taken by prepare.
.br
Exits 2 when refs changed (1 before). After a passing verify the run records RUN/verified, and later verify and clean compare with that: your own fast\-forwards of develop and master and of agent|feature|release|hotfix/* branches, and new branches there, are accepted; remote refs, tags, deletions and rewrites still fail. Run it on every run before merging any of them.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "clean " "RUN_DIR"
Remove a collected, verified run and its temporary directory.
.br
Prepare makes a private temporary directory for the run (TMP, on /dev/shm when it exists) that the agent puts its test sandboxes in; clean removes it with the clone, the bare copy and the output. It refuses a run whose output has no manifest (collect first) and one whose repository refs changed (that is evidence).
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-discard
also clean a run whose output holds no manifest.
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi supervise
Find stalled agent runs: notify, prepare a nudge, never act alone.
.PP
Hooks only record (the run\-event verbs of qargi hook append ids, times, a tool name and a hash to a per\-run file under the state directory; no command, message or error text). One idempotent tick is the only actor: it folds those files, the local\-run registry and the session tracker into runs.json and classifies each unfinished run in a fixed order: waiting on an approval (not a stall), stopped by an API error, interim (turn ended, background work running, a cap of about 35 minutes), looping (three identical tool digests in a row), suspected dead (no activity for too long); for cloud sessions requires_action, budget_reached, stale while running, idle awaiting review. It acts only by printing `QARGI SUPERVISE <class> <run> <next\-action>` lines, appending to supervisor.log and the audit log (origin supervisor), and PREPARING a nudge in nudges.jsonl. A nudge is a fixed template, at most 2 per run with at least 10 minutes between them, never while the run waits on an approval or has little budget left, and never an approval. A script cannot message a local subagent: the coordinating session delivers the prepared nudge and records it with delivered, and only then does it count. A cloud nudge is sent only by nudge, which needs \-\-asked. Nothing sends automatically; the thresholds are data in lib/supervise\-defaults.json, conservative floors until `qargi report` shows real run lengths. Run the tick on a timer or a loop; status warns when it has not run for twice its interval.
.RS
.TP
.B tick
Fold the events, classify the runs, print what needs attention, prepare nudges.
.br
Run twice with nothing new it prints nothing the second time.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
act as of this time (2026\-10\-30T12:00:00Z); for tests and replays.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the result as JSON.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B status
Show the runs by class and the nudges waiting; warns when the tick is overdue.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
compare the last tick with this time.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-json
print the registry as JSON.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "nudge " "SID"
Send the fixed nudge to a cloud session, only when the caps allow.
.br
Refused when the session waits on an approval or has little budget left, when 2 nudges were already counted, or within 10 minutes of the last one. The message is the fixed template, never text from the agent.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-asked " "REQUEST"
the request, in your words, that names the cloud instance; recorded in the audit log.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
act as of this time; for tests and replays.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "delivered " "KEY"
Record that you delivered a local run's prepared nudge.
.br
KEY is the agent id the tick printed. Only then does the nudge count against the cap.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-now " "ISO"
record this delivery time; for tests and replays.
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B hooks
Print the settings.json hook snippet for the run\-event handlers.
.br
It never writes the file: merge the entries into your Claude Code settings.json yourself.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-qargi " "COMMAND"
the command to put in the snippet (default qargi).
.RE
.RE
.SS Review and apply
.TP
.B qargi review
Check what an agent returned and put it on a task branch.
.RS
.TP
.BI "check " "RUN_DIR"
Check a run: numbered patches, messages, manifest, groups.
.br
Refuses a monolithic patch, a commit without a message body and a manifest that does not match the patches. A review run passes with a report.md that has a Findings section instead of patches.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-empty
accept a manifest with no commits (a team member that was blocked or had nothing to change).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "apply " "RUN_DIR"
Apply a collected, checked run to a task branch of the repository.
.br
Refuses a run applied already, and unless review check passes for RUN_DIR/out (warnings allowed) and local verify accepts the run (a refs\-changed result prints the diff). Creates the branch at the commit the run's base branch had when the run was prepared and applies every patch of out/patches in order with git am, in a temporary worktree of the project's repository: your checkout is never touched and no other ref changes. The committer is the one the repository has configured, else the author of the patches. It refuses a branch that exists; a patch that does not apply removes the worktree and the branch and names the patch. On success it prints the commits and the next commands, and records branch and tip in RUN_DIR/applied and the branch in the run's ref snapshot, so local clean still accepts the run.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-branch " "BRANCH"
the branch to create (default agent/<slug> of the run).
.RE
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B prune
List, or with \-\-yes delete, the agent/* branches already merged into develop or a feature.
.br
A branch is merged when it is an ancestor of develop or of any feature/* branch (the test git branch \-d makes, against that target rather than the checked\-out branch). Only local agent/* branches are considered; a branch checked out in a worktree is kept; no other branch is touched. Prints each branch it lists or deleted.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-project " "PROJECT"
the project, instead of the one inferred from here.
.RE
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-yes
delete them (without it nothing changes).
.RE
.RE
.TP
.B qargi team
Run a task with a lead and role agents (opt\-in).
.PP
For a change that affects usage: the build member changes the code, the write member updates the operational docs, the review member judges both. The lead is this Claude Code session, a cloud lead, or a session started in the console; all follow the lead contract. Each member returns its own series against its own base.
.RS
.TP
.BI "brief " "[PROJECT] TEAM"
Print the brief a lead needs for one task.
.RS
.TP
.BI "\-\-task " "TEXT"
what to do.
.RE
.RS
